  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Significant rain event will continue for northwest Montana\n   throughout the day on Tuesday.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning valley\n   fog and chilly mornings. Chance of frost for high valleys of\n   southwest Montana Tuesday and Wednesday mornings.\n\n-  30 to 40 percent chance for another wet and even colder system\n   next weekend with high elevation snow along the Continental \n   Divide\n\n\nThe low pressure system that has brought widespread precipitation\nto north central Idaho and western Montana has shifted into north\ncentral Montana this afternoon. With this progression of the low,\nthe steady moderate to heavy rainfall has shifted northwards and \nis now generally occurring across northwest Montana (north of \nHighway 200). Localized one hour precipitation rates of 0.3 to 0.4\ninches have been observed and will continue to be possible into \nthis evening. Flood watches remain in effect for portions of \nnorthwest Montana as an additional 1 to 2 inches is anticipated \nthrough mid day Tuesday. Minor flooding in poor drainage areas, \nponding water on roadways and rock/debris on roadways is a \nconcern, along with rapidly rising small streams and creeks.\n\nSouth of Highway 200, precipitation will be more showery in nature\nthrough this evening before decreasing after sunset. Showers will\nbe capable of brief heavy rain, but this activity is not\nanticipated to be widespread. \n\nLocations across southwest Montana may see enough clearing tonight\nto allow temperatures to drop into the mid 30s, possibly allowing\nfor some frost development. Elsewhere, cloud cover should limit\novernight cooling. \n\nA drying trend comes into play late Tuesday into Wednesday.\nSeasonable temperatures in the mid 70s for many valley locations\nare anticipated. Chilly mornings and even some fog will feel very\nfall like to end the week.\n\nBeginning on Thursday, winds will increase from the west as zonal\n(westerly) flow develops ahead of the next approaching weather \nsystem. This could cause some increased chop on local lakes as \nwell as possibly enhancing fire weather concerns across far \nsouthwest Montana, which has largely been missed by the recent \nprecipitation event.\n\nForecast models still differ on how the next upper level trough\nwill evolve over the Northern Rockies by next weekend. A trend to\ncooler and showery weather looks likely no matter the scenario. \nHowever, 30 to 40 percent are beginning to show a cold and wet low\npressure system over the region. If this were to occur, another \nround of widespread precipitation and even high elevation snow \ncould be a possibility.",
